 # Living Survey: multi_agent                                                                                        
-                                                                                                                    
-## Introduction                                                                                                     
-                                                                                                                    
-This document collects the scientific literature regarding **multi_agent**.                                         
-                                                                                                                    
-## Multi-Agent Architectures and Orchestration                                                                      
-Recent advancements have focused on scalable and heterogeneous multi-agent orchestration. The MOSAIC platform       
introduces a universal agent-level interface that enables heterogeneous agents (RL policies, LLMs, VLMs, and humans) 
to operate within shared reinforcement learning environments via an IPC-based worker protocol, facilitating          
reproducible cross-paradigm comparisons [^https:__openalex.org_W7133365089]. Similarly, Project Synapse employs a    
hierarchical multi-agent architecture orchestrated by LangGraph, where a central Resolution Supervisor delegates     
tactical subtasks to specialized workers for autonomous last-mile delivery disruption resolution                     
[^https:__openalex.org_W7124227974]. In software engineering, multi-agent LLM architectures have been adapted for    
automated front-end generation, coordinating generation, validation, and repair loops to synthesize React            
applications from design artifacts and user stories [^https:__openalex.org_W7166959618].                             
-                                                                                                                    
-## Agent Governance, Trust, and Safety                                                                              
-As multi-agent ecosystems scale, governance and trust mechanisms become critical. The Agent Trust Fabric (RFC-ATF-5)
proposes a Cognitive Governance Layer that addresses decision-space verification through counterfactual governance   
engines and establishes universal governance invariants aligned with major regulatory frameworks                     
[^10.5281_zenodo.20391722]. Trust quantification is further explored through information-theoretic approaches,       
demonstrating how entropy and mutual information can measure trust in multi-agent cooperative navigation and         
federated learning pipelines under deceptive conditions [^10.6084_m9.figshare.32101069]. Safety remains a            
foundational architectural property; the "Internet of Agents" framework deconstructs systemic vulnerabilities across 
single agents, multi-agent systems, and interoperable ecosystems, advocating for co-designed safety protocols        
alongside capability expansion [^10.48550_arxiv.2512.00520]. Security and authorization boundaries are rigorously    
tested by APort Vault, which replays thousands of human-written attacks against live payment agents to evaluate      
policy configurations and the Open Agent Passport specification, demonstrating near-zero unauthorized transfers      
behind the authorization layer [^2609.22076]. Containment strategies are formalized through Provisional Reachability,
which holds every agent crossing in escrow for auditing and revocation, mathematically bounding information leakage  
and preventing secret assembly across windows [^2609.21957]. Policy-regulated multi-agent systems are structured by  
the JustAct framework, providing a Rocq specification and Rust implementation for open-multi-agent environments with 
enforceable policy constraints [^10.5281_zenodo.21240014]. Enterprise governance is further refined by the Agent     
Steward concept, which identifies context provenance and temporal validity as a missing architectural dimension to   
detect silent errors caused by obsolete agent context, introducing the Context Validity Declaration to make          
obsolescence detectable at runtime [^10.5281_zenodo.21660204].                                                       
-                                                                                                                    
-## Multi-Agent Systems in Specialized Domains                                                                       
-Multi-agent paradigms are increasingly deployed across diverse verticals. In medical intelligence, a                
state-machine-based multi-agent architecture enables collaborative diagnostic reasoning by having specialized agents 
analyze evidence, vote, and revise conclusions, significantly improving accuracy on multimodal datasets              
[^10.3390_math14142562]. Bio-robotics leverages evidence-grounded multi-agent systems to translate application       
requirements into genetic circuit designs, automating module assembly and conflict checking across extensive         
biological part libraries [^10.48550_arxiv.2608.19699]. In educational assessment, multi-agent processing combined   
with NLP clustering automates the categorization and grading of student submissions, reducing manual workload while  
maintaining human-in-the-loop oversight [^10.54254_2755-2721_2025.30694]. Additionally, conversational multi-agent   
frameworks are being utilized to evaluate and refine prompts across different LLM families, leveraging collaborative 
agent interactions to surface prompt weaknesses more effectively than single-model testing [^10.5120_ijca2025925510].
The integration of agents into enterprise and specialized workflows demands rigorous evaluation and value alignment. 
EnterpriseVal introduces a use-case-level evaluation system that maps metric vectors with confidence bounds to       
REJECT/CONDITIONAL/SCALE decisions, closing the gap between public benchmarks and deployment readiness [^2609.21841].
In industrial settings, LLM-based systems for root cause analysis of test failures are compared in single-agent      
versus orchestrated multi-agent configurations, revealing that single-agent setups currently offer faster, lower-cost
reporting for practical baseline deployment [^2609.21843]. User-centric design is advanced through Value-Sensitive   
Delegation, which analyzes tens of thousands of user posts to show that human values are primarily fulfilled at the  
operating conditions set around agent runs rather than the outputs themselves [^2609.22067]. Social agent realism is 
explored by investigating personality-tuned LLMs, finding that while fine-tuning improves linguistic diversity,      
inter-rater agreement limits confidence in behavioral fidelity improvements [^2609.21857]. Finally, TrialAtlas       
deploys a memory-augmented multi-agent research organization for clinical trial design, coordinating specialized     
agents for literature synthesis and regulatory precedent analysis to outperform baselines in detecting trial         
deficiencies and predicting regulatory success [^2609.21859].                                                        
-                                                                                                                    
-## Error Attribution and Reliability                                                                                
-Debugging complex multi-agent interactions requires robust error attribution methods. The ECHO algorithm introduces 
a hierarchical context representation combined with objective consensus voting to pinpoint agent-level and step-level
failures in interaction traces, outperforming binary search and all-at-once evaluation strategies                    
[^10.48550_arxiv.2510.04886]. In systems engineering, multi-agent root-cause diagnosis frameworks construct evidence 
graphs from system logs to autonomously identify kernel crash origins, demonstrating the efficacy of distributed     
agent collaboration in complex debugging pipelines [^10.5281_zenodo.19247220]. Finally, quantum and neuromorphic     
computing techniques are being integrated into multi-agent reinforcement learning to safely explore large solution   
spaces, enhancing the reliability and explainability of optimal control in autonomous robotics [^2408.03884].        
-                                                                                                                    
-## Agent Memory and Reasoning Mechanisms                                                                            
-Recent research has significantly advanced how agents manage, retrieve, and reason over information. Designer-RSI   
introduces a continual adaptation framework where a frozen frontier model accumulates and refines reusable procedural
memories from user traffic, widening its skill bank through successful and failed executions without weight updates  
[^2609.22086]. To improve the reliability of memory retrieval, the Memory Decision Layer (MDL) proposes a            
zero-parameter controller that fuses relevance, reliability, and task risk to explicitly decouple confidence from    
consistency, drastically reducing hallucination rates under conflicting memories [^2609.22043]. Long-term            
conversational memory is further addressed by AutoViewMem, which organizes memories into self-configuring,           
low-overlap semantic views to prevent semantic interference and improve top-K retrieval accuracy [^2609.21940]. In   
interactive settings, RAVEL employs retrieval-aware online reinforcement learning to optimize question generation    
policies, dynamically reallocating questioning budgets toward attributes that yield the most useful retrieval        
evidence [^2609.21924]. Furthermore, opinion dynamics in social simulations are made auditable and controllable      
through Bayesian Chronicle Agents, which separate belief states from linguistic output and update stances via single 
Bayesian steps per utterance [^2609.21997].                                                                          
-                                                                                                                    
-## Agent Environments and Training Paradigms                                                                        
-The development of robust agents relies heavily on scalable environments and targeted training methodologies.       
MintAct unifies UI grounding, multi-step navigation, and visual tool use across mobile, desktop, and web platforms,  
leveraging a scalable reinforcement learning infrastructure that maintains stability under noisy feedback and        
off-policy drift [^2609.22083]. For software development, CodeMidas scales agentic coding environments by            
transforming implemented functionality in open-source codebases into executable RL tasks, improving performance      
across issue repair, whole-program construction, and terminal work benchmarks [^2609.22068]. Hybrid computer-use     
agents are evaluated in RecreationWorld, a five-platform framework that provides reproducible environments with      
native GUI control and coding tools, using running references as oracles for hidden behavioral tests [^2609.22000].  
In health interventions, an agentic Just-in-Time Adaptive Intervention (JITAI) system autonomously reviews personal  
sleep and behavioral data to adapt reminders and interventions, demonstrating technical feasibility for personalized 
health support [^2609.21805].                                                                                        
-                                                                                                                    
-## Multi-Agent Control, Robotics, and Strategic Games                                                               
-Coordinated multi-agent systems continue to push boundaries in physical and strategic domains. Multi-Agent          
Event-Triggered LQG Control addresses shared communication constraints by decoupling optimal control laws across     
agents while formulating a globally coupled transmission schedule as a mixed-integer linear program, optimized via a 
receding-horizon scheduler [^2609.21974]. In robotics, MAAP enables collaborative manipulation by treating every     
robotic arm as a dual-purpose active perception device, predicting role-dependent actions within a single network to 
